\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on four English NER benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient, each serving a distinct role.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} (551 test instances, 6 entity types) is the primary medium-difficulty test bed where models achieve ${\approx}$65\% F1.
\textbf{Few-NERD} \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (INTRA coarse; 37{,}648 instances, 8 entity types, ${\approx}$75\% F1) provides a low-degeneracy contrast where LP selection succeeds (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (3{,}453 test sentences, 4 entity types, ${\approx}$91\% F1) serves as a ceiling-effect control where limited oracle headroom constrains selection gains.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (1{,}287 social media instances, 52\% zero F1) probes the signal-collapse boundary where model capability is insufficient.

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
\textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are LoRA fine-tuned using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} (training details in Appendix~\ref{app:training}; Few-NERD uses 3 epochs vs.\ 5 for others due to earlier convergence; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
We draw $N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary) with vLLM constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}, with $N{=}16$ for scaling experiments (Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}
\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{NER quality estimation ($\rho$ and AUROC; $N{=}8$, primary seed${=}42$; 3-seed stability $\sigma{\leq}0.026$ in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). Best in \textbf{bold}. RE results in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}. $N{=}16$ in Table~\ref{tab:robustness}.}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
VC & Structural & \textbf{.379} & \textbf{.663} & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
SJ  & Structural & .360 & .650 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
FK & Surface & .267 & .602 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
EM  & Surface    & .295 & .614 & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
\midrule
LP     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents $\rho$ and AUROC for five signals across tasks and models ($\varepsilon$-free; $\varepsilon{=}0.05$ applies to selection only, stable across $[0, 0.2]$, Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon_robustness}).

\paragraph{Signal rankings are difficulty-dependent.}
On SciERC (medium difficulty), structural signals lead (VC $\rho{=}0.379$, SJ $0.360$) over LP ($0.205$); bootstrap CIs confirm the gap ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).
On CoNLL (single-sample F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads both $\rho$ and AUROC ($0.816$).
At the signal-collapse boundary (WNUT-17, macro F1${\approx}$42\%), full-set correlations are dominated by the zero/non-zero boundary (VC $\rho{=}0.724$ but conditional $\rho{=}0.038$; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}); LP shows the reverse (conditional $\rho{=}0.350$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}).
This collapse illustrates a regime where model capability is insufficient to produce meaningful inter-sample variation: 52\% of instances yield zero F1 across all samples, and consistency signals merely detect this floor rather than discriminating quality among non-trivial outputs.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{The Correlation-Selection Gap}
\label{sec:selection_gap}

Four of five signals (SJ, FK, EM, VC) assign identical scores to all candidates from the same input by construction (\S\ref{sec:formulation}); they can estimate quality across instances but cannot rank candidates within an instance.
The empirical question is whether LP, the sole per-sample signal, can exploit its candidate-discriminative nature for selection.
LP selection effectiveness is primarily determined by LP-quality alignment rather than degeneracy level (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ NER selection (macro F1) on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$). LP is per-sample; others use consensus-proximity. Best non-oracle in \textbf{bold}. Non-tied subset in \S\ref{sec:discussion}; RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.775 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
Greedy ($T{=}0$) & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Per-sample signal}} \\
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Instance-level signals (consensus-proximity selection)}} \\
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .67 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & --- \\
\midrule
Random           & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_selection_gap}
\caption{The correlation-selection gap on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, 2-seed mean). Bars show quality estimation $\rho$; the line with error bars shows $\Delta$F1 vs.\ greedy. Despite $\rho$ up to 0.36, no signal significantly outperforms greedy (all $p{>}0.05$; Table~\ref{tab:selection}). Dashed green: oracle upper bound ($+13.1$~pp); dotted gray: random baseline.}
\label{fig:selection_gap}
\end{figure}

Instance-level signals assign identical QE scores to all candidates; MBR conversion does not improve over greedy on the primary test bed (Table~\ref{tab:selection}; Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}).
Instance-level signals predict \textit{which inputs} the model handles reliably, not \textit{which output} is best.

\paragraph{LP: the sole per-sample signal also fails.}
On SciERC ($n{=}529$, 2-seed sign tests\footnote{MDE${\approx}$1.5~pp at 80\% power ($n{=}529$); Few-NERD ($n{=}32{,}565$; MDE${\approx}$0.22~pp) provides the definitive test.}), the oracle (0.775) reveals 13.0~pp of headroom, but LP achieves $+0.2$~pp ($p{=}0.29$), below the MDE (${\approx}$1.5~pp).
The pattern holds across CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}); under stringent TOST anchors, some configurations show significant \textit{degradation} (Appendix~\ref{app:tost}).
Few-NERD provides the definitive contrast: LP achieves $+1.39$~pp ($p{<}0.001$) at low degeneracy (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}), while structural signals still degrade selection.

\paragraph{Expected selection gain decomposition.}
The expected selection gain can be approximately factored as $\mathbb{E}[\Delta\text{F1}] \approx (1 - d) \cdot \Delta_{\text{oracle}} \cdot g(\rho_{\text{within}})$, where $d$ is the degeneracy rate, $\Delta_{\text{oracle}}$ is the oracle headroom, and $g$ is assumed monotonically increasing in within-instance LP-F1 correlation $\rho_{\text{within}}$, reflecting that stronger alignment enables more informative candidate ranking.
This decomposition is consistent with the observed data: SciERC ($d{=}17.8\%$, $\Delta_{\text{oracle}}{=}13.1$\,pp, $\rho_{\text{within}}{=}0.262$) predicts a small gain (actual: $+0.04{\pm}0.48$\,pp, 3-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$; Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}); Few-NERD ($d{=}13.8\%$, $\Delta_{\text{oracle}}{\approx}8$\,pp, $\rho_{\text{within}}{=}0.667$) predicts the largest gain (actual: $+1.39$\,pp); CoNLL ($d{=}48.4\%$, $\Delta_{\text{oracle}}{\approx}2$\,pp, low $\rho_{\text{within}}$) predicts near-zero gain (actual: $-0.85$\,pp).
The high SciERC seed variance ($\sigma{=}0.48$\,pp) reflects weak LP-quality alignment ($\rho{\approx}0.26$), consistent with the bound predicting unreliable selection.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Cross-Dataset Contrast: Few-NERD}
\label{sec:fewnerd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{LP selection across datasets (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, gold-filtered). Degen.\%: fraction with identical F1 across all samples. $p$: paired bootstrap vs.\ greedy. Few-NERD/CoNLL: 3-seed means; SciERC: single-seed (3-seed: $+0.04{\pm}0.48$~pp; Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). Protocol details in \S\ref{sec:evaluation}; epoch choices in Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}.}
\label{tab:fewnerd}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccccr@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & $n$ & Greedy & Degen.\% & LP $\rho$ & LP $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & 2{,}756 & .908 & 48.4\% & .302 & $-$0.85\,pp & 1.0 \\
SciERC & 529 & .644 & 17.8\% & .205 & $+$0.61\,pp & .193 \\
Few-NERD & 32{,}565 & .748 & 13.8\% & .220 & $\mathbf{+1.39}$\,pp & ${<}.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) confirms that LP selection effectiveness is inversely associated with degeneracy.
Under standard early stopping, Few-NERD degeneracy is 13.8\% and LP achieves $+1.39$~pp ($p{<}0.001$), while all structural signals degrade selection (VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$~pp); LP is 50$\times$ more seed-stable ($\sigma{=}0.04$ vs.\ SJ $2.02$~pp) and generalizes to LLaMA-3.1-8B (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).
Training convergence modulates degeneracy: 5-epoch Few-NERD raises degeneracy to 31\% and halves LP gains (Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).

\paragraph{Degeneracy alone does not predict LP selection direction.}
Across 23 configurations, degeneracy does not predict LP gain ($\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though within fixed settings it causally modulates LP (Few-NERD per-entity-type $\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).
Cross-dataset prediction requires LP signal quality: Few-NERD (within-instance $\rho{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$) shows consistent gains, while SciERC ($\rho{\approx}0.26$, even at matched duration $\rho{=}0.319$; Table~\ref{tab:epoch_scierc}) does not.
Conditional analysis on the favorable subset yields $+2.47$~pp on SciERC but remains negative on CoNLL (Appendix~\ref{app:conditional_decomp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain across 23 configurations ($N{=}8$; $\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$). Within Few-NERD per-entity-type analysis ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}), degeneracy predicts LP gain when alignment is held constant.}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

LP selection gains scale with $N$ (Few-NERD $N{=}16$: $+0.52$~pp, $p{=}0.003$; Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}); structural signals remain negative regardless of sample budget.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robustness_main}

Consistency signals are robust across $T{\in}\{0.7, 1.0\}$ with sublinear $N$-scaling gains (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}); LP shows the largest temperature sensitivity ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.044$).
Signal rankings are stable across seeds (Kendall's $W{=}1.0$ at $N{=}8$; $m{=}3$ raters, interpret with caution given low $m$; exact $p{\approx}0.028$; Table~\ref{tab:ranking-stability}).
NER calibration is reasonable (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Scale ablation: Qwen3-8B vs.\ 4B NER quality estimation ($\rho$; $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, seed${=}42$). 8B values identical to Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}; 4B uses matched evaluation protocol. RE scale ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:scale_ablation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll cc r@{}}
\toprule
& & \textbf{8B} & \textbf{4B} & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & SJ & .436 & .422 & $-$.014 \\
      & FK & .400 & .418 & $+$.018 \\
SciERC & SJ & .360 & .422 & $+$.062 \\
       & FK & .267 & .326 & $+$.059 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Scale robustness.}
With Qwen3-4B (Table~\ref{tab:scale_ablation}), QE is comparable (mean $|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$), yet selection remains below greedy despite 13.0~pp headroom. Task difficulty is a stronger predictor than model scale within the 3B--8B range (Appendix~\ref{app:llama3b}).
Degeneracy scales with capability: 8B shows 17.8\% vs.\ 4B's 10.4\% on SciERC (Table~\ref{tab:degeneracy}).

\paragraph{LoRA-rank ablation.}
LoRA rank${=}8$ (vs.\ 32) lowers degeneracy to 7.2\% but LP remains below greedy ($\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){\approx}0.24$); temperature manipulation ($T{=}1.2$; \S\ref{sec:discussion}) provides a cleaner diversity test, confirming that low degeneracy and adequate alignment are independently necessary.

\paragraph{Supervised signal combination.}
Supervised classifiers (logistic regression, MLP, random forest; 5-fold CV, 3-seed mean, $N{=}16$) and a DeBERTa-v3-base verifier encoding input-output pairs all fail to close the selection gap: selection F1 remains at or below greedy (Appendix~\ref{app:supervised_combination}).

Hidden state probes (MLP on final-layer representations) achieve higher quality correlation than LP on SciERC ($\rho{=}0.388$ vs.\ $0.283$, $+37$\%) but do not improve selection (gap closure $-5.1$\% vs.\ $+4.8$\%; Appendix~\ref{app:hidden_probe}). The correlation-selection gap extends to model-internal representations.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Prescriptive Results}
\label{sec:prescriptive}

The preceding sections establish that instance-level selection fails for structured IE. We now present two approaches that extract value from multi-sample generation without relying on sample ranking.

\paragraph{Entity-level construction.}
Rather than selecting one of $N$ complete outputs, we aggregate entities across all samples. For each unique entity mention, we compute its LP-weighted or uniform frequency across $N$ samples and retain those exceeding a confirmation threshold $\theta{=}2/N$ (an entity is included if it appears in ${\geq}2$ of $N$ samples). This threshold is tuning-free across all datasets ($\theta{=}0.5$ majority baseline in Appendix~\ref{app:entity_merge}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Entity-level construction vs.\ greedy decoding ($\Delta$ macro F1; $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, Qwen3-8B, matching Tables~\ref{tab:selection}--\ref{tab:fewnerd}). LP: LP-weighted entity aggregation; Unif.: frequency-based. $\theta{=}2/N$ (tuning-free). $p$: paired bootstrap vs.\ greedy. Best per dataset in \textbf{bold}.}
\label{tab:entity_construction}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & Wt. & Greedy & Constr. & $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
SciERC & LP & .644 & $\mathbf{.661}$ & $+$1.67\,pp & .004 \\
       & Unif. & .644 & .659 & $+$1.54\,pp & .023 \\
\addlinespace
Few-NERD & LP & .748 & $\mathbf{.757}$ & $+$0.88\,pp & ${<}.001$ \\
         & Unif. & .748 & .751 & $+$0.32\,pp & .003 \\
\addlinespace
CoNLL & Unif. & .908 & .904 & $-$0.39\,pp & n.s. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

LP-weighted construction significantly improves over greedy on SciERC ($+1.67$\,pp, $p{=}0.004$) and Few-NERD ($+0.88$\,pp, $p{<}0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:entity_construction}). CoNLL shows no significant gain ($-0.39$\,pp), consistent with ceiling-regime degeneracy. The mechanism is recall-driven: aggregating low-frequency correct entities across samples recovers mentions that no single sample captures.
LP-weighted aggregation outperforms uniform voting at $N{=}8$ (SciERC: $+1.67$ vs.\ $+1.54$; Few-NERD: $+0.88$ vs.\ $+0.32$\,pp): generation probability carries useful information for entity weighting even when it fails for instance-level selection.
Increasing $N$ from 8 to 16 further improves construction (Few-NERD: $+0.88{\to}+1.32$\,pp), as more samples provide broader entity coverage; at $N{=}16$, LP-weighting and uniform converge, consistent with vote averaging dominating at higher $N$.

\paragraph{Construction and LP selection are complementary.}
Construction dominates on SciERC ($+1.67$\,pp vs.\ LP selection $+0.61$\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:selection}), where LP-quality alignment is weak (within-instance $\rho{\approx}0.2$).
On Few-NERD, LP selection outperforms construction ($+1.39$\,pp vs.\ $+0.88$\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}), where alignment is strong ($\rho{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$).
The choice is governed by LP-quality alignment: when LP tracks entity-level F1 within instances, per-sample ranking succeeds; when alignment is weak, entity aggregation bypasses ranking.

\paragraph{LP-alignment gating.}
We probe 50 held-out validation instances to estimate within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1})$. Above threshold $0.3$, LP selection is applied; below it, entity construction is used instead. Adjusted $\rho$ scores on the dev split (SciERC $0.167$, CoNLL $0.065$, Few-NERD $0.516$) correctly route all three datasets, with dev-set routing matching test-set routing 3/3. Two caveats apply: (a) the protocol is validated on three datasets with large inter-dataset margins; (b) on high-degeneracy datasets (CoNLL, valid ratio 22\%), the adjusted score magnitude is noisy but the routing decision remains robust due to wide margin from the $0.3$ threshold.

\paragraph{Selective prediction.}
Orthogonal to construction, QE signals support selective prediction: at 80\% coverage, retaining high-confidence instances yields $+5$--$8$\,pp F1 (AUPRC 0.70--0.92; VC strongest on Qwen, model-dependent on LLaMA; Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).
When selection fails, QE scores route low-confidence instances to human review.

